!! Why Separate Note-taking and Flashcard Creation?

Writing notes and creating flashcards are two different cognitive activities.

When writing notes, I prefer to focus entirely on understanding and organizing knowledge: exploring ideas, explaining concepts in my own words, and building connections between them.

Having to stop and think about flashcard questions and answers in the middle of this process can be distracting. It breaks the flow of writing and forces me to constantly switch between two different ways of thinking.

That's why I prefer to separate these activities.

First, focus on understanding the material and organizing your notes. Once everything is clear, move on to a dedicated flashcard creation session.

At this stage, you can concentrate entirely on reviewing what you've written, identifying what is worth remembering, and turning those ideas into questions and answers.

This allows you to stay in a flow state while creating flashcards, instead of constantly interrupting your writing process.

!!! From Understanding to Remembering

I prefer to understand something before trying to memorize it.

For me, learning is about connecting new information with existing knowledge, gradually turning external information into something we genuinely understand and can use.

AI can generate plenty of information, notes, and even flashcards. But generating information is not the same as internalizing it.

The important question is not how much information we can collect, but how much of it we can actually make our own.

This is why I find it useful to separate understanding from memorization, while keeping them connected through a deliberate workflow.

!! How It Works

!!! 1. Write and organize knowledge in TiddlyWiki

Start by writing your notes as you normally would.

Explore ideas, explain concepts in your own words, collect references, and connect related information.

Focus on understanding first. You don't need to think about flashcards while writing.

!!! 2. Create flashcards with TiddlyFlashcards

Once your notes are ready, open the Card Creator in the current Tiddler.

Focus on extracting the knowledge worth remembering, and turn it into questions and answers.

A single Tiddler can contain multiple flashcards, using different Anki note types.

You can preview, edit, delete, and suspend or unsuspend cards independently.

Each card remains associated with its original Tiddler, so you can always return to the complete context when needed.

!!! 3. Sync with Anki

Synchronize your flashcards with Anki.

TiddlyWiki remains your primary place for authoring and managing flashcards, while TiddlyFlashcards handles synchronization with Anki.

!!! 4. Review with Anki

Let Anki handle spaced repetition and review scheduling.

Focus on recalling what you've learned and gradually strengthen the memories you've chosen to retain.

!! A Simple Division of Responsibilities

| !Component | !Main responsibility |
|TiddlyWiki |Knowledge authoring and organization(Keeping your flashcards context) |
|TiddlyFlashcards |Focused flashcard creation and synchronization |
|Anki |Spaced repetition and review |

Rather than replacing either tool, TiddlyFlashcards connects their strengths into a single learning workflow.

!! Further Reading

If you're interested in the ideas behind this workflow, I'd highly recommend reading:

* [[20 Rules of Knowledge Formulation — SuperMemo Guru|https://supermemo.guru/wiki/20_rules_of_knowledge_formulation]]

Written by Piotr Wozniak, this article discusses how to formulate knowledge for effective long-term learning.

